1. Derivation
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ohos-kernel-port is a derivative work.  It is derived from the kernel build
and packaging system of the ophub project:

    ophub/kernel            https://github.com/ophub/kernel
    ophub/amlogic-s9xxx-armbian   https://github.com/ophub/amlogic-s9xxx-armbian
    Copyright (C) 2021 ophub contributors
    Copyright (C) 2021 https://github.com/unifreq/openwrt_packit
    Copyright (C) 2021 https://github.com/ophub/kernel

    Licensed under the GNU General Public License, version 2.

The kernel source trees that ophub builds on are maintained by unifreq:

    https://github.com/unifreq/linux-6.6.y  (and 5.10.y, 5.15.y, 6.1.y,
                                            6.12.y, 6.18.y, 5.10.y-rk35xx,
                                            6.1.y-rockchip)

    Licensed under GPL-2.0 WITH Linux-syscall-note.

ohos-kernel-port differs from the above as follows.  Per GPL-2.0 section 2(a),
the modified files carry prominent notices stating the change and its date;
the substantive changes are:

  * Kernel source acquisition now resolves the OpenHarmony kernel repositories
    (kernel_linux_6.6, kernel_linux_5.10, kernel_linux_4.19, kernel_linux) on
    gitcode.com and checks out the matching OpenHarmony-<x.y>-Release branch,
    instead of resolving unifreq/linux-<series>.y and ophub/linux-<series>.y.
  * Kernel configuration now consumes the OpenHarmony configuration repository
    (kernel_linux_config), supporting both OpenHarmony's hand written fragments
    (base_defconfig, type/{small,standard}_defconfig) and its complete
    generated per-board configurations, instead of ophub's
    kernel-config/<board>/config-<version> fragments.
  * An explicit lane matrix (data/ohos-kernel-lanes.tsv) replaces ophub's
    implicit series/board resolution, so that every buildable combination of
    OpenHarmony release branch, kernel version, system type and board is named
    and reproducible.
  * Version stamping is off by default.  ophub injects a signature into every
    kernel it builds; OpenHarmony does not require it, so it is opt-in.
  * The Armbian / OpenWrt / FnNAS userland integration is removed.  The
    integration target is the OpenHarmony standard system.
  * Packaging defaults to producing OpenHarmony boot artifacts and can
    delegate to the OpenHarmony kernel_linux_build repository.


